var _page_default$729 = _template("Gh", (input) => {
	_scope_reason();
	_scope_id();
	docs_article_default({
		title: "Introduction",
		description: "marko-ui is a set of accessible, copy-paste components for Marko 6, styled with Tailwind v4 and driven by Zag.js state machines. Open Source. Open Code.",
		href: "/docs",
		toc: TOC$8,
		content: _content("Gh0", () => {
			_scope_reason();
			_scope_id();
			_html(`<p><strong>This is not a component library. It is how you build your component library.</strong></p><p>marko-ui is a port of <a href=https://ui.shadcn.com target=_blank rel=noreferrer>shadcn/ui</a> to Marko 6. It keeps the idea that made shadcn/ui work — you own the component source — and swaps the React/Radix half for Marko tags backed by <a href=https://zagjs.com target=_blank rel=noreferrer>Zag.js</a> state machines. There are ${_escape(COMPONENTS$1.length)} components in the registry today.</p><ul><li><strong>Open Code:</strong> the component source lands in your repo, not in node_modules.</li><li><strong>Composition:</strong> every component is a Marko tag with attribute tags for its parts.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> a shadcn-compatible registry served over HTTP, installed with the shadcn CLI.</li><li><strong>Behavior from Zag:</strong> keyboard handling, focus management, and ARIA come from framework-agnostic machines.</li><li><strong>Resumable:</strong> components are written to survive Marko's serialize-and-resume model, not just to render.</li></ul><h2 id=open-code>Open Code</h2><p>Installing a component copies its <code>.marko</code> files into <code>src/components/ui/&lt;name&gt;/</code> in your project. There is no <code>@marko-ui/components</code> package to import from, and no wrapper layer to fight when a design calls for something the default styling does not do.</p><ul><li><strong>Full transparency:</strong> the markup, the classes, and the machine wiring are all right there.</li><li><strong>Easy customization:</strong> change the Tailwind classes in the file; nothing overrides you.</li><li><strong>AI-friendly:</strong> a model reading your repo sees the real component, not a compiled dependency.</li></ul><p>The tradeoff is the same one shadcn/ui makes: upstream fixes to <em>styling</em> do not flow to you automatically. Behavior fixes largely do, because the behavior lives in the <code>@zag-js/*</code> packages you install from npm, and those you can bump like any other dependency.</p><h2 id=composition>Composition</h2><p>Multi-part components use Marko's attribute tags rather than a separate exported subcomponent per part. A dialog is one import and a nested structure, which keeps the parts scoped to the parent that owns their state.</p>`);
			code_block_default({
				language: "marko",
				code: DIALOG_EXAMPLE
			});
			_html("<p>The <code>|props|</code> parameter on <code>@trigger</code> is how a part hands its machine props to whatever element you choose to render — spread them onto a <code>&lt;Button&gt;</code>, an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, or anything else. It is marko-ui's equivalent of Radix's <code>asChild</code>.</p><p>Simple controls follow Marko's two-way binding convention: state props are bound with <code>value:=</code> or <code>checked:=</code>, and every Zag <code>onXxxChange</code> callback is also exposed as a plain <code>xxxChange</code> attribute when you want the callback form.</p>");
			code_block_default({
				language: "marko",
				code: USAGE_EXAMPLE
			});
			_html("<h2 id=distribution>Distribution</h2><p>The registry is published as static JSON in the <a href=https://ui.shadcn.com/schema/registry.json target=_blank rel=noreferrer>shadcn registry-item format</a>, which means the official <code>shadcn</code> CLI installs marko-ui components without knowing anything about Marko. Every file ships as a plain <code>registry:file</code> with an explicit target path, so the CLI never runs its React-specific transforms over our templates.</p>");
			code_block_default({
				language: "bash",
				code: "bunx shadcn@latest add https://marko-ui.saulo.tech/r/switch.json"
			});
			_html("<p>See <a href=/docs/installation>Installation</a> for project setup and <a href=/docs/cli>CLI</a> for the full command reference.</p><h2 id=behavior-from-zag>Behavior from Zag</h2><p>Zag.js implements each interactive widget as a framework-agnostic state machine that already handles roving focus, typeahead, dismiss layers, scroll locking, and the ARIA attributes each pattern requires. marko-ui binds those machines into Marko through the <code>marko-ui</code> package, so an accordion behaves identically here and in the React, Vue, or Solid ports of the same machine.</p><p>A practical consequence: when you customize a component you are editing markup and classes, not keyboard handling. It is hard to break accessibility by restyling.</p><h2 id=resumability>Built for Resumability</h2><p>Marko 6 resumes a page by <em>serializing reactive state</em> — it does not re-run your template in the browser the way React hydration does. Zag's service and API objects contain functions from npm code and cannot be serialized, so marko-ui components pass machines and APIs around as getters rather than as raw values. This is the single constraint that shapes most of the component internals, and it is documented in detail in <a href=/docs/creating-components>Creating Components</a>.</p><h2 id=faq>FAQ</h2><h3>Do I need Zag.js knowledge to use marko-ui?</h3><p>No. Every Zag prop is surfaced as a normal Marko attribute on the component, and the defaults are set for you. Zag knowledge matters when you write a <em>new</em> component, not when you use one.</p><h3>Can I use marko-ui with plain Marko, without @marko/run?</h3><p>Yes. The components are ordinary Marko tags and only need Marko 6, Tailwind v4, and the relevant <code>@zag-js/*</code> packages. @marko/run is what this documentation site uses and what the installation guide assumes, but nothing in the components depends on it.</p><h3>How do I get upstream component updates?</h3><p>Re-run <code>bunx shadcn@latest add</code> for the component with <code>--overwrite</code>, then diff. Because the files are yours, this is a merge you perform deliberately rather than a version bump that silently changes your UI.</p>");
		})
	});
});
