var _page_default$723 = _template("tk", (input) => {
	_scope_reason();
	_scope_id();
	docs_page_default({
		title: "How to Create a Marko-Zag Component",
		description: "The complete recipe, using Switch — the reference implementation — as the worked example.",
		content: _content("tk0", () => {
			_scope_reason();
			_scope_id();
			_html("<section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">The mental model</h2><p>A marko-ui component is three layers: a <strong>Zag machine</strong> supplies behavior and accessibility, three <strong>marko-ui tags</strong> wire it into Marko's resumability model, and <strong>Tailwind classes</strong> supply the look. The one rule everything else derives from: Marko resumes pages by <em>serializing reactive state</em> — it never re-runs your template in the browser. Zag's service and api objects contain functions from npm code and cannot be serialized, so they must never become reactive state or cross a tag boundary as raw values. Template-written closures <em>can</em> be serialized, which is why everything below passes machines and apis around as <strong>getters</strong>.</p></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">1. Name the files</h2><p>A component lives in <code>ui/&lt;name&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.marko</code> (never <code>index.marko</code> — tabs and conversations need real names). Multi-part components add one file per part in the same directory (<code>header.marko</code>, <code>content.marko</code>…) plus <code>variants.ts</code> when there are cva variants, and <code>registry.meta.json</code> for registry metadata.</p></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">2. Type the Input</h2><pre class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border bg-muted/50 p-4 text-sm\"><code>import * as switchMachine from \"@zag-js/switch\";\nimport type { MachineInput } from \"marko-zag\";\n\nexport type Input = MachineInput&lt;\"input\", switchMachine.Props&gt; &amp; {\n  checkedChange?: (checked: boolean) =&gt; void;\n};</code></pre><p><code>MachineInput&lt;Tag, Props&gt;</code> is the native tag's attributes intersected with the machine's full <code>Props</code>, with only <code>id</code> made optional (it is generated). Everything Zag supports is automatically part of your API. Add one <code>xxxChange</code> prop per Zag <code>onXxxChange</code> callback — it enables Marko's two-way bind shorthand (<code>&lt;Switch checked:=state/&gt;</code>) and receives the plain value; Zag's original callback remains available and receives the details object.</p></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">3. Wire the machine — three tags</h2><pre class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border bg-muted/50 p-4 text-sm\"><code>&lt;machine-props/machineProps from=input pick=switchMachine.props\n  onCheckedChange(details: switchMachine.CheckedChangeDetails) {\n    input.onCheckedChange?.(details);\n    input.checkedChange?.(details.checked);\n  }/&gt;\n&lt;service/service machine=() =&gt; switchMachine.machine props=machineProps/&gt;\n&lt;connect/api=(service, normalizeProps) =&gt;\n  switchMachine.connect(service, normalizeProps)\n  service=service\n/&gt;</code></pre><ul class=\"list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-sm\"><li><strong><code>&lt;machine-props&gt;</code></strong> builds the machine's props closure: picks machine-owned props out of <code>input</code> by name (<code>pick=switchMachine.props</code> — the machine's exported name array; a split <em>function</em> would be unserializable tag input), injects a generated stable <code>id</code>, and merges every attribute you write on the tag. Callback adaptations go here, visibly — they are the component's public contract. Type the <code>details</code> params with the machine's exported types.</li><li><strong><code>&lt;service&gt;</code></strong> creates and owns the running machine on the client; during SSR it stays unstarted and connect reads a throwaway instance — which is how correct <code>aria-*</code>/<code>data-*</code> attributes appear in server HTML before any JS runs. Controlled props re-notify automatically: the tag tracks whatever your <code>machineProps</code> closure reads.</li><li><strong><code>&lt;connect&gt;</code></strong> returns <code>api</code> as a <strong>getter</strong>. Call it at every use site: <code>api().getRootProps()</code>. The getter has fresh identity per machine update, so every spread that reads it recomputes.</li></ul><p class=text-sm>Both <code>machine=</code> and the <code>&lt;connect&gt;</code> value <em>must</em> be closures written in your template. Passing <code>switchMachine.machine</code> or <code>switchMachine.connect</code> directly throws <code>Unable to serialize \"input\"</code>.</p></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">4. Render with api() spreads</h2><pre class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border bg-muted/50 p-4 text-sm\"><code>&lt;label ...api().getRootProps() data-slot=\"switch-label\" class=\"...\"&gt;\n  &lt;span ...api().getControlProps() data-slot=\"switch\" class=cn(\"...\", input.class)&gt;\n    &lt;span ...api().getThumbProps() data-slot=\"switch-thumb\" class=\"...\"/&gt;\n  &lt;/span&gt;\n  &lt;input ...nativeAttrs() ...api().getHiddenInputProps()&gt;\n&lt;/label&gt;\n\n&lt;return=api&gt;</code></pre><ul class=\"list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-sm\"><li>Faithful shadcn Tailwind classes; semantic tokens only — never raw colors.</li><li>Every part carries a <code>data-slot</code>; style states via Zag's <code>data-[state=...]</code> attributes.</li><li>Native pass-through (<code>aria-*</code>, <code>data-*</code>, form attributes) = <code>splitProps(input)[1]</code> minus <code>class</code> and your <code>xxxChange</code> props, spread onto the appropriate native element.</li><li><code>&lt;return=api&gt;</code> exposes the live api getter to parents.</li></ul></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">5. Multi-part components: attr tags, not arrays</h2><p>When a component repeats a part per caller-supplied entry (tabs triggers, accordion items, menu items…), prefer an <strong>attr-tag compound API</strong> over an <code>items=[...]</code> array. Marko has no context/provider primitive, so per-part sibling tags (Radix-style <code>&lt;TabsTrigger&gt;</code>) can't share the parent's live <code>api</code> getter safely — a caller who forgets to thread it gets either a 500 or a silently unwired, inaccessible part. Attr tags avoid the problem entirely: they're <em>data</em> collected by the parent's own render, so the api getter never crosses a tag boundary.</p><pre class=\"overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border bg-muted/50 p-4 text-sm\"><code>export interface TriggerAttrs { value: string; disabled?: boolean; content?: Marko.Body }\n\nexport type Input = MachineInput&lt;\"div\", tabsMachine.Props&gt; &amp; {\n  items?: TabItem[];                              \n  trigger?: Marko.AttrTag&lt;TriggerAttrs&gt;;      \n  content?: Marko.Body&lt;[string]&gt;;\n};\n\n\n&lt;const/triggerTags=[...(input.trigger ?? [])]/&gt;\n\n\n\n&lt;const/triggers=(triggerTags.length &gt; 0 ? triggerTags : (input.items ?? []).map((item) =&gt; ({ value: item.value, disabled: item.disabled })))/&gt;</code></pre><p class=text-sm>Call site: <code>&lt;Tabs&gt;&lt;@trigger value=\"one\"&gt;One&lt;/@trigger&gt;...&lt;/Tabs&gt;</code>. Keep <code>items=</code> working unchanged for the data-driven case (a select bound to fetched options should stay array-driven, not compound) — both paths render through the same normalized list, so there's no branching duplicated downstream.</p><p class=\"text-sm font-medium\">Landmine: wrap ternaries used as a `&lt;const&gt;` value in parens.</p><p class=text-sm><code>&lt;const/x=a &gt; 0 ? a : b/&gt;</code> is broken two different ways depending on formatting, and Marko does not always catch it at compile time:</p><ul class=\"list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-sm\"><li>A multi-line ternary with indented continuation lines fails to compile: <code>Line has extra indentation at the beginning</code>.</li><li>A single-line ternary with a bare <code>&gt;</code> comparison is <strong>ambiguous attribute syntax</strong> — Marko may parse the tag as ending at that <code>&gt;</code>, silently truncating the expression. This does not always throw: it can compile clean and just assign the wrong runtime value (e.g. a length number instead of the ternary result), breaking a downstream <code>&lt;for&gt;</code> with <code>must be an iterable, but received `3`</code>.</li></ul><p class=text-sm>Always wrap the whole ternary in parens on one line: <code>&lt;const/x=(a &gt; 0 ? a : b)/&gt;</code>. Re-verify by curling the route and grepping rendered output after any edit to a normalization <code>&lt;const&gt;</code> — a 200 status alone does not prove the value was computed correctly.</p></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">6. Overlay components</h2><ul class=\"list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-sm\"><li>Floating content goes in <code>&lt;portal&gt;&lt;if=api().open&gt;...</code> — portal outside, condition inside (stable host, no orphan accumulation).</li><li>Popper-positioned parts (popover, tooltip, menu, select…) spread <code>...api().getPositionerProps()</code> then add STATIC <code>style=positionerStyle</code> (from marko-ui): Zag positions via imperative <code>--x/--y</code> CSS vars that a reactive style attribute would wipe.</li><li>Never wrap caller-supplied triggers in your own <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> — nested buttons get un-nested by the HTML parser and corrupt hydration. Use a render-prop: <code>trigger: Marko.Body&lt;[Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;]&gt;</code>, rendered as <code>&lt;${input.trigger}(api().getTriggerProps())/&gt;</code>.</li></ul></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">7. Verify</h2><ul class=\"list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-sm\"><li><code>curl</code> the demo route: 200, SSR carries the expected <code>aria-*</code>/<code>data-state</code> attributes, and no <code>Unable to serialize</code> anywhere.</li><li>Real-browser check: interactions need window focus (machines often require focus-then-click); synthetic <code>element.click()</code> can miss.</li><li>Formatter landmine: prettier-plugin-marko can turn multi-line attribute arrows into block bodies <em>without a return</em> — keep attribute closures single-expression and re-verify after formatting.</li></ul></section><section class=space-y-3><h2 class=\"text-lg font-semibold\">Reference</h2><p class=text-sm>The living version of all of this is <code>packages/registry/default/ui/switch/switch.marko</code> — copy it. Adapter internals: <a class=underline href=/docs/zag-adapter>the Zag adapter anatomy</a>.</p></section>");
		})
	});
});
