			code_block_default({
				language: "bash",
				code: EJECT_COMMAND
			});
			_html("<p>It reads which components are actually in use from <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code>'s installed <code>ui/</code> tree, cross-checks that list against the registry, then fetches and writes local source for every one of them — the exact same fetch-and-write path <code>add</code> uses, so the result is indistinguishable from having run <code>add</code> for each component by hand. It then flips <code>components.json</code>'s <code>distribution</code> field to <code>copy</code>.</p><p><code>eject</code> does not remove the <code>@marko-ui/shadcn</code> npm dependency or the CSS block <code>init --distribution import</code> wrote — the command prints both as manual follow-up steps, since your project keeps rendering correctly with the old CSS in place until you remove it and reinstall the copy-path theme (<code>marko-ui add style</code>).</p><h2 id=mu-hooks>mu-* styling hooks</h2><p>Every marko-ui component carries semantic <code>mu-*</code> classes in its markup — <code>mu-button</code>, <code>mu-accordion-trigger</code>, and so on — regardless of which distribution you're on. On <code>copy</code> these are inert decoration; the generated Tailwind utility classes sitting alongside them do the actual styling, and editing that file is how you change anything.</p><p>On <code>import</code> they're load-bearing: <code>mu-*</code> is the public styling API a consumer's own stylesheet targets. Scope an override to one visual style by combining it with the <code>style-&lt;name&gt;</code> class your CSS entry activates:</p>");
